1. Architectural Blueprint: Enterprise MERN Stack
While MERN (MongoDB, Express, React, Node.js) is highly agile, healthcare systems require strict ACID compliance for financial transactions (claims) and flexible schemas for medical records (EHR).

Frontend (React.js): Build a unified Single Page Application (SPA) or micro-frontends. Use Redux or Context API for state management. Implement heavily modular components so the UI can adapt dynamically based on the user's RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) token.

Backend (Node.js & Express.js): Move away from a standard monolith. Design a Microservices Architecture or a highly decoupled modular monolith. You will need distinct services: Auth Service, Integration/FHIR Middleware Service, Claims Engine, and Inventory Routing Service.

Database (MongoDB): MongoDB is excellent for storing complex, nested FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources) JSON documents from e-Ubuzima or OpenClinic. However, for the insurance claims and financial ledgers, you must utilize MongoDB’s multi-document ACID transactions (introduced in v4.0+) to ensure no data is lost during network failures, or hybridize by adding a SQL database specifically for billing.

API Gateway & Interoperability Layer: This is the heart of ImitiNet. Your Node backend must act as an API gateway that translates incoming data from ISHYIGA (pharmacies) and OpenClinic into a standardized format before distributing it to RSSB/MMI.

2. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Module Design
Your authentication layer (using JWT and bcrypt) must strictly segregate these four portals.

A. Patient Portal
National ID SSO: Log in using the Rwandan National ID.

Geospatial Medicine Search: Use MongoDB's $near spatial queries to allow patients to search for a prescribed drug and find the closest pharmacies with active stock.

Insurance Engine: Real-time API call to RSSB/MMI returning their active coverage tier and calculating their out-of-pocket co-pay before they even leave the house.

EHR Viewer: A read-only dashboard pulling recent consultation notes and e-prescriptions synced from e-Ubuzima.

B. Pharmacy & Hospital Portal
Smart Inventory Webhooks: Instead of manual entry, build secure endpoints that ISHYIGA and local hospital POS systems can ping automatically whenever a drug is dispensed, keeping your central MongoDB ledger updated in real-time.

Claim Submission Engine: Automate the packaging of dispensed e-prescriptions into structured claim payloads formatted specifically for RSSB/MMI APIs.

Operational Dashboards: React charts (e.g., Chart.js or Recharts) showing daily dispensing volumes, pending claim statuses, and stock-out warnings.

C. Insurer Portal (RSSB/MMI)
Claims Analytics: High-level dashboards rendering thousands of daily transactions.

Fraud Detection Logic: Implement algorithmic checks on the backend (e.g., flagging if a single National ID is prescribed the same heavy antibiotic from three different clinics in one week).

Onboarding Workflow: A digital pipeline to verify, approve, and issue API keys to newly registered pharmacies joining the ImitiNet network.

D. Regulator Portal (MoH / Rwanda FDA)
Traceability Ledger: Track serialized batches of medicine from import to patient dispensing to combat counterfeit drugs.

Macro Health Metrics: Heatmaps showing disease outbreaks (e.g., a sudden spike in antimalarial sales in a specific province) to inform public health policy.

3. Execution Roadmap
Aligning with your proposal document, here is how a senior engineering team executes this timeline:

Phase 1: Architecture & Core Build (Months 1–3)

Define the JSON/FHIR data schemas for interoperability.

Set up CI/CD pipelines (GitHub Actions, Docker containerization) for reliable deployment.

Develop the core Node.js API Gateway and the database schemas.

Build the base React interfaces for the 4 portals.

Milestone: A sandbox environment where dummy data flows from a simulated OpenClinic to a simulated RSSB endpoint.

Phase 2: Pilot & Integration Testing (Months 4–6)

Develop the exact API bridges for RSSB and MMI.

Deploy locally in a controlled pilot (e.g., a few select pharmacies and one district hospital).

Stress-test the system's ability to handle concurrent searches and claim submissions.

Milestone: The first real-world e-prescription is tracked, dispensed, and claimed entirely through ImitiNet.

Phase 3: National Rollout & Scale (Months 7–12)

Optimize database indexes and implement Redis caching for the most frequent search queries (like common painkillers or antibiotics) to reduce server load.

Begin heavy user training and onboarding for pharmacists and hospital admins.

Lock down security protocols (penetration testing, ensuring compliance with Rwanda's data protection laws regarding sensitive patient health data).

Phase 4: Regional Expansion & Analytics (Year 2+)

Refactor code for multi-tenancy to support neighboring East African regulations.

Introduce predictive data analytics for insurers and regulators using the massive historical dataset you’ve accumulated.